{"name":"Blue Flame","hex":"#005e88","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"flame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible part of fire; a stream of burning vapour or gas, emitting light and heat.","A romantic partner or lover in a usually short-lived but passionate affair."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brilliant reddish orange-gold colour, like that of a flame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Flame","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Flame is a rocket-powered land speed racing vehicle that was driven by Gary Gabelich and achieved a world land speed record on Bonneville Salt Flats in Utah on October 23, 1970. The vehicle set the FIA world record for the flying mile at 622.407 mph (1,001.667 km/h) and the flying kilometer at 630.388 mph (1,014.511 km/h). Blue Flame's world record speed stood for thirteen years before being exceeded by Thrust2 in 1983."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Goodwood2007-121_The_Blue_Flame.jpg/330px-Goodwood2007-121_The_Blue_Flame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Flame","wikidata":"Q515737"},{"title":"Blue Flame (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Flame is a 1993 American independent sci-fi film written and directed by Cassian Elwes, starring Kerri Green as one of two seductive aliens who live inside the head of a renegade police officer."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Flame_(film)","wikidata":"Q4929114"},{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/330px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame","wikidata":"Q235544"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg/500px-Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg/1280px-Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg/500px-Technik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATechnik_Museum_Sinsheim%2C_Blue_Flame.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/1280px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spectrum of the blue (premixed, i.e., complete combustion) flame from a butane torch showing molecular radical band emission and Swan bands. Virtually all the light produced is in the blue to green region of the spectrum below about 565 nanometers, accounting for the bluish color of sootless hydrocarbon flames.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/1280px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different flame types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In zero-G, convection does not carry the hot combustion products away from the fuel source, resulting in a spherical flame front.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACandlespace.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA